#########
# DO NOT EDIT THIS FILE
# Managed by Ansible
######
# Pins the OpenNMS packages this role installs, so a routine `apt upgrade`
# cannot move them across a major version. Installing already requests an exact
# version; without a pin nothing constrains the package afterwards.
#
# Three tiers, because a single exact-version pin expires. The repository serves
# one point release per dist, so `Pin: version 36.0.3*` is certain to stop
# matching once 36.0.4 ships — and a preferences file whose only entry has
# expired behaves identically to no preferences file at all. Measured: with the
# pin expired and a second major's source enabled, `apt upgrade` moved
# opennms-common from 35.0.5-1 to 36.0.3-1, exactly as it did with no pin.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. APT applies the *first* matching entry, not the highest
# priority one. Putting the origin entry above the others makes every version
# land at -1, and `apt install opennms-common` then reports no installation
# candidate. Keep these most-specific-first.
#
# Packages are named explicitly rather than globbed as `opennms-*`: a glob would
# silently capture any future package sharing the prefix, including ones this
# role does not install and whose pinning nobody decided on. The negative tier
# especially must stay scoped — `preferences.d` is read in alphabetical order and
# first-match-wins spans files, so a `Package: *` entry here would shadow the
# tiers written by the other installing roles on a single-host stack.
#
# rrdtool, jrrd2 and iplike are deliberately absent, for one good reason and
# one weaker one. rrdtool is an ordinary Debian package, so pinning it would
# block security updates to protect something that is not OpenNMS — the wrong
# trade. jrrd2 and iplike are published only by debian.opennms.org and exist in
# no Debian suite, so there is no security stream to preserve for them; they are
# excluded because they are not OpenNMS itself, not because pinning them would
# cost anything. Whether they should be pinned anyway is an open question.
#
# To upgrade deliberately: change opennms_version and re-run. This file is
# rewritten before the packages are installed, so the new version is permitted
# in the same run — there is no separate unpin step.

# Tier 1 — the configured version, while the repository still carries it.
# Priority 1001 rather than 1000: above 1000 permits a downgrade, so pinning
# back to an earlier release does what the operator asked instead of silently
# doing nothing.
#
# `-*` matches Debian revisions of this release and nothing else. A bare
# trailing `*` is an fnmatch glob over the whole version string, so
# `opennms_version: 36.0.1` would also match 36.0.10 through 36.0.19 — and at
# priority 1001 apt applies that as a forced change, which is the opposite of
# what this tier is for.
Package: {{ opennms_pinned_packages | join(' ') }}
Pin: version {{ opennms_version }}-*
Pin-Priority: 1001

# Tier 2 — the same major, once the configured version has been superseded and
# removed. Deliberately below 1000: this fallback should not acquire the
# downgrade power that tier 1 has, since nobody asked for it. The cost of this
# tier is that `apt upgrade` may move a *patch* after the pinned one disappears;
# the alternative was moving a whole major, which is what this file exists to
# stop.
#
# Keyed on the major itself rather than on the repository's dist. A dist-keyed
# form (`release n=opennms-NN`) has to be told which dist to expect, and any
# divergence between that value and the dist the source line actually points at
# — an overridden `opennms_repo_dist`, a mirror publishing a different Codename
# — makes this tier silently match nothing, dropping every version to tier 3's
# -1 and leaving the package with no installation candidate at all once tier 1
# expires. The version glob cannot diverge: it is derived from the same
# `opennms_version` as tier 1.
Package: {{ opennms_pinned_packages | join(' ') }}
Pin: version {{ opennms_version.split('.')[0] }}.*
Pin-Priority: 900

# Tier 3 — every other OpenNMS release. -1 makes a version ineligible for
# automatic selection, so no major jump can happen on its own. It is not a lock:
# an explicit `apt-get install <pkg>=<version>` still installs through it, which
# is why this role's own versioned install task is unaffected.
#
# Keyed on the repository's published `Origin: OpenNMS` metadata, NOT on
# `Pin: origin debian.opennms.org`. In apt_preferences the bare `origin` form
# matches the site *hostname*, so pointing opennms_repo_url at a mirror would
# silently disable this tier — no error, no failed task, just no protection.
Package: {{ opennms_pinned_packages | join(' ') }}
Pin: release o={{ opennms_pin_origin }}
Pin-Priority: -1
